# The three test tiers, in one place, because two copies drift.
#
# Read by the Makefile (`include tests/tiers.env`) and by
# .github/workflows/ci.yml, which greps the assignments into $GITHUB_ENV.
# Nothing else may spell a tier filter; a guard test asserts both readers derive
# from this file, and that neither contains a hand-written one.
#
# NOT sourceable shell, despite looking like it (#490). `. tests/tiers.env`
# dies on the unquoted `&` in the filters, and AUTONATE_TIER_SERVICES would try
# to run `Dapr` as a command. Both real readers are fine -- make's `include` and
# a grep are not shell evaluation -- but do not write `source tests/tiers.env`
# in a script and expect it to work.
#
#   slim          no RequiresService trait          what GitHub runs
#   full-local    everything except Keycloak        a developer's machine
#   full-keycloak everything                        future
#
# VSTest has no trait-ABSENCE operator, so "no trait" is the negated conjunction
# of every known service. That is why AUTONATE_TIER_SERVICES exists beside the
# filters rather than the filters being written out by hand: a service added to
# the list without being added to the slim filter would silently move its tests
# into slim, and a guard test derives one from the other.

# Every service a RequiresService trait may name. A trait carrying anything else
# fails a guard, because a typo would otherwise land its test in slim.
AUTONATE_TIER_SERVICES=Flowable Dapr Keycloak

# Derived from the line above. Kept literal rather than computed because
# `dotnet test --filter` is passed through several layers of quoting and a
# computed string is one shell away from being wrong in a way nothing notices.
AUTONATE_TIER_SLIM_FILTER=RequiresService!=Flowable&RequiresService!=Dapr&RequiresService!=Keycloak
AUTONATE_TIER_FULL_LOCAL_FILTER=RequiresService!=Keycloak
AUTONATE_TIER_FULL_KEYCLOAK_FILTER=

# --- Size pins (#453) ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# What each filter must discover in tests/AutoNate.E2E.Tests. Checked by
# infra/tier-integrity.sh at the end of `make test-full-local`, because a tier
# that silently gets smaller reports success for the wrong reason.
#
# EXACT, not floors. House style (ExecutionOracleSizeTests' 29, the coverage
# ratchet): growth has to be as visible as loss, or the number drifts upward
# and stops meaning anything. When you add tests, move the pin in the same
# commit -- that is the point, not the friction.
#
# Per service as well as per tier, because the tier total cannot see a test
# re-traited OUT of Flowable: it stays in full-local, it just stops needing the
# engine. That is exactly how a live-engine oracle gets quietly defanged.
#
# Measured 2026-09-15 at ddb58e9: 172 + 198 + 1 = 371, + 5 Keycloak = 376.
# full-local moved to 386 the same day by #474, which added 15 untraited
# BpmnDiagramHelperTests. The service pins stayed put -- that is the shape of a
# healthy growth: tests joined slim, nothing left Flowable.
# Then 203/403 by #471, which proved the three activity-marker rows: +3 cells
# and +2 negative controls under Flowable, +26 untraited diagram-helper tests.
# Then 208/414 by #522, the oracle-instrument story: the effect vocabulary gained
# `instance-starts` and `host-cancelled`, and each new name arrives with BOTH a
# negative control and -- because no manifest row declares it yet -- a positive
# one, so +4 under Flowable. The 3 untraited AttachedHostOf cases joined slim
# (202 -> 205), and the backend gained the reason floor and the two pairing
# guards (2729 -> 2732). No row declares either new effect here; that is the
# eleven coverage stories' work, one ratchet step each.
# The first slice of M4d landed at 223/440/216/2733. Then #523 added the signal
# broadcast route: +4 Flowable (the live wake, fan-out, refusal and scope
# complement) and +11 slim backend (6 broadcaster unit tests that need no engine,
# 5 gate tests). The split is deliberate -- the decision rules are unit-testable
# and belong where a merge can see them; only "a broadcast moves instances" needs
# Flowable.
# Then 210/416 by #526, which proved the two conditional rows. #525 moved nothing
# because it retired #522's two positive controls as it declared the two effects
# they stood in for; there was nothing left to retire here, so these are +2.
# The notes above are per-STORY and were written as each landed, which is why
# "Then 210/416 by #526" sits BELOW "The first slice of M4d landed at
# 223/440/216/2733" and reads backwards. They then stopped entirely, while the
# pins kept moving (#550). What actually landed, by merge, as
# Flowable / full-local / slim-e2e / slim-backend:
#
#   #538  first slice   c3be2b1   223 / 440 / 216 / 2733
#   #539  second slice  b3e2845   230 / 447 / 216 / 2744
#   #542  final slice   034630c   234 / 451 / 216 / 2763
#   #543                ddb75dd   235 / 452 / 216 / 2764
#
# The per-story notes explain the why of each step; this is the ledger they were
# being checked against, and a pin whose history is unwritten is a number nobody
# can audit -- which is the same failure the pins themselves exist to prevent.
#
# M4d's fix pass (#544, #546-#550) moved four of the seven. FLOWABLE 235 -> 238:
# the two row-level negative controls #546 was about (escalation and conditional
# boundaries, non-interrupting) and #544's live proof that an unpublished draft
# declares nothing. SLIM_E2E 216 -> 223: #549's five RoutesByScript cases and
# #548's two paging cases -- the latter deliberately in the E2E project but
# UNTRAITED, because the helper it drives lives there and the tier that blocks a
# merge is where a paging regression has to be visible. SLIM_BACKEND 2764 ->
# 2771: #549's two snapshot-site prefix cases plus its theory's second row,
# #547's two dispatcher warnings and #544's two broadcaster draft cases.
# Round two of the M4d fix pass (#552-#555) moved three. SLIM_BACKEND 2771 ->
# 2785: #552's store guard for the published-version join, #553's three registry
# guards, and #554's five prefix-site theories (10 rows -- every one of #482's
# seven sites is now reached, which took two goes because both #549 and its own
# fix miscounted in the same direction). SLIM_E2E 223 -> 224 and FULL_LOCAL
# 462 -> 463: #555's throwing-page case. FLOWABLE is unchanged at 238 -- this
# round added no live-engine test, which is itself the shape to notice: the
# blocker it closed was a missing guard, not a missing proof.
AUTONATE_TIER_COUNT_FLOWABLE=238
AUTONATE_TIER_COUNT_DAPR=4
AUTONATE_TIER_COUNT_KEYCLOAK=5

# #524 took Dapr from 1 to 3, and that is the pin doing something new. Until then
# the Dapr trait covered one page-load test; the queue-start pair is the first
# thing in the suite that actually PUBLISHES to the bus and asserts the app acted
# on it. Both carry Flowable too, because starting a workflow needs an engine --
# so the same two tests move both service pins, which is correct rather than
# double counting: each pin asks "how many tests need THIS service".
AUTONATE_TIER_COUNT_FULL_LOCAL=463

# Tests needing MORE THAN ONE service, counted once here so the identity
# SLIM_E2E + FLOWABLE + DAPR - MULTI_SERVICE = FULL_LOCAL stays exact (#524).
#
# The identity assumed every E2E test needs at most one service, which was true
# until one needed two: the queue-start pair publishes to the bus AND starts a
# workflow, so it carries both traits, lands in both service pins, and lands once
# in full-local. Each service pin still answers exactly the question it should --
# "how many tests need THIS service" -- and the arithmetic learns about the
# overlap rather than the traits being bent to keep the arithmetic simple.
#
# PINNED, not computed. A term derived from the other pins would absorb the drift
# the identity exists to catch: FLOWABLE=150 would pass again by calling the
# missing 84 an overlap. `The_multi_service_pin_matches_the_classes_that_carry_two_traits`
# counts it from the source.
AUTONATE_TIER_COUNT_MULTI_SERVICE=3

# --- Slim's own pins (#476) ---------------------------------------------------
#
# The tier that actually blocks merges had neither a skip check nor a count
# floor, while full had both. Checked inside GitHub's gate, not only in
# `make test-slim`: a developer's machine is not the thing that blocks a merge.
#
# Per project, because they fail differently. The backend shards run UNFILTERED
# (ci.yml applies the slim filter to the E2E project only, and no backend test
# carries a RequiresService trait), so its pin catches deletion. The E2E pin
# also catches a trait being added to a slim class: the filter stops matching
# it, the count drops, and the gate is red until the pin moves in the same
# commit -- which is the whole point, since that shrink used to be invisible.
#
# Re-measured 2026-09-18 at 962be85 by discovery, and again after the round-two
# fix pass. The line here used to read `Measured 2026-09-15 at d34829d` while
# the numbers below had moved three times since (#555): a provenance line that
# names the wrong commit is worse than none, because it reads as checked.
AUTONATE_TIER_COUNT_SLIM_BACKEND=2785
AUTONATE_TIER_COUNT_SLIM_E2E=224
